   "l": "A small state, few people —",
   "v": true,
   "i": "A small state, and few people in it.",
   "n": "Four characters, two adjectives and two nouns, and no verb at all. English has to choose whether this is a description ('the state is small') or a prescription ('make the state small'). Wang Bi takes it as a description and says why: if even a small state with few people can be brought back to the old way of living, how much more a great one — the chapter takes the easy case on purpose. The whole picture that follows hangs off this one unpunctuated line.\n\nThe Mawangdui manuscripts read 小邦寡民, 'a small domain'. 邦 was the personal name of Liu Bang, the founder of the Han, and after his death the character was replaced everywhere by 國 — the same kind of taboo substitution as 恆 for 常 in chapter 1. The received text still carries the Han court's caution.",
   "p": true
  },
