   "l": "Let there be implements of tens-and-hundreds, and yet not use them;",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Let them have gear enough for tens and hundreds, and never put it to use;",
   "n": "什伯之器 is genuinely disputed and the English above is kept vague on purpose. 什 and 伯 are the file of ten and the company of a hundred — the units people were mustered into — so one reading makes this the gear of an army, weapons and kit. The other takes it as implements that do the work of ten or a hundred men, labour-saving machines; the Mawangdui manuscripts write 十百人之器, 'implements of ten or a hundred men', which is read as support for that — though it will bear the military sense just as well. Wang Bi does not decide: he says only that the people have such implements and no use for them.\n\n伯 is a case of the graph and the word parting company. The character's own reading is bó ('eldest brother, a chief'), but the word written with it here is 百 bǎi, 'a hundred', so bǎi is what we print — as with 梲 for 銳 in chapter 9, the sign on the page is one thing and the word in the mouth another."
