   ],
   "l": "Though there be boats and carriages,\nthere is nowhere to ride them;\nthough there be armour and weapons,\nthere is nowhere to draw them up.\nLet people go back to knotting cords and using them,\nfind their food sweet,\ntheir clothes fine,\ntheir dwellings restful,\ntheir customs a delight.\nNeighbouring states look across at one another,\nthe sound of fowl and dogs is heard from one to the other,\nthe people reach old age and death\nand do not go back and forth with one another.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Boats and carts they have, and nowhere to take them; armour and weapons they have, and nowhere to marshal them. Let people go back to keeping their accounts in knotted cord: their food tastes good to them, their clothes look fine, their houses are restful, their customs a pleasure. The next state is close enough to see, and the cocks and dogs of it close enough to hear, and the people grow old and die without ever having gone back and forth between the two.",
   "n": "Three patterns, all of them everywhere in the language once you have seen them here.\n\n無所乘之: 所 stands in front of a verb and makes a noun of it — 'that for which one rides'. So 無所乘之 is 'there is nothing for which to ride them', which is not the same as saying they cannot ride: the boats are there, the occasion is not.\n\n相 before a verb makes it reciprocal — 'X and Y —— each other'. 相望 is 'look across at one another', 相聞 'are within hearing of one another'. It is a single character doing the work of English 'each other', and it goes before the verb, not after. The chapter's last line has a third one — 不相往來, 'do not go back and forth with one another' — and the reciprocal is the whole point of it.\n\nAnd 甘其食，美其服，安其居，樂其俗 is four adjectives used as verbs of judgement in a row: 'sweet their food' means 'they find their food sweet'. That is the reading Heshanggong takes, and it makes the point of the chapter — nothing has been improved, only the appetite for improvement has gone. A causative reading is possible for the same four characters ('make their food sweet to them'), and Chinese does not choose between them.\n\n結繩 is the record-keeping that came before writing: knots tied in cord to keep an account. Going back to it is not nostalgia for stupidity but for a scale of life small enough that a knot was enough.\n\nWhere the received text has 使人, the Mawangdui manuscripts read 使民, 'let the people' — the same word as in the two units above. It changes nothing but the register."
